    <!-- Explanations shown under the cooling comfort and fire weather details -->
    <string name="d_cool_explainer">Your body cools by sweating. The wet-bulb temperature combines heat and humidity to show how well that sweat can evaporate: the closer it gets to skin temperature (~35 °C), the less cooling you get. Above ~31 °C even healthy people struggle to stay cool for long.</string>
    <string name="d_fire_explainer">The Fosberg Fire Weather Index rates how quickly a fire could spread from the weather alone: heat and dry air dry out fine fuels like grass, and wind feeds the flames. It doesn\'t know how dry the ground has been over recent weeks, so always follow official fire danger ratings and bans.</string>

